{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md", "content": "# Separating markers from a stamp template

For a grid with scattered single-color marks and a distinctive multicolor shape, map the output delta against the input before deciding the shape's role. Record each copied footprint's exact coordinates and colors, then compare those to both scattered markers and any instance embedded in the source template. The template's own center may or may not be an active marker; infer this from the demo, rather than assuming all similar-colored cells are equivalent.

Define one explicit anchor and offsets for every template cell, including vertical/asymmetric parts. Enumerate marker positions from the original input before applying any edits. Replay on each demo and compare the full output, including unchanged cells, overlap, clipping, and whether the source template persists. A visually plausible generic cross is not a substitute for the observed exact template.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/template-stamp-marker-separation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md", "content": "# Review: heterogeneous demonstrations without a validated common rule

This instance supplied three sharply different pairs: a 21×21 sparse diagonal-color scene became a full 5-color periodic field; a 23×23 otherwise blank grid with two bottom stripes became an alternating two-color row pattern; and a 2×2 near-uniform patch had its lone zero filled. The 12×12 test instead contained offset diagonal tracks in colors 8, 2, 6, and 7. The demonstrations were evidence of radically different surface transformations, but no cumulative exact-delta analysis produced a validated shared selector or output mapping. Serial demo requests (three were used) did not resolve the relationship; the last action continued requesting rather than making an evidence-based decision.

Reusable lesson: task IDs and stream membership alone do not justify assuming a common pixel-level operation. Record each pair's exact delta and output structure before requesting another. Explicitly test whether the task may be an abstract transformation shared across different visual classes; formulate at least two candidate abstractions and derive predictions that can be replayed against all examples. If no candidate maps all demonstrations exactly, another request is justified only if its predicted outcomes discriminate those candidates. Do not invent a test output by blending the demo patterns or select one demo's operation based on superficial resemblance. Keep the action protocol strict: one JSON action only on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-recovery", "file": "references/task-29b1b5eb-instance-review.md", "content": "# Task 29b1b5eb: fresh-instance evidence caution

This review involved a new stream instance bearing a task ID that had appeared in historical skill references. The prior note suggested rightward extensions but explicitly warned it was not a validated universal rule. Transferring that hypothesis directly led to unvalidated output; repeated requests were made without an explicit discriminator or a cumulative pair-by-pair delta ledger. The resulting grid submission was reported as an invalid ARC grid, and the attempted retry repeated the same malformed shape.

A fresh task instance requires independent evidence. Treat same-ID references as historical warnings, not transformation rules. For every returned pair, immediately record exact changed coordinates and unaffected candidates, update named hypotheses, and request again only for an observation that separates plausible rules. When invalid-grid feedback arrives, check row count, widths, dimensions, values, and action serialization before making another attempt; keep format diagnosis separate from inference. This session did not establish a reliable transformation for its test, so this reference documents process failures and recovery gates, not a solved output rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-evidence-recovery/references/task-29b1b5eb-instance-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-task-36d8805c-review.md", "content": "# Session review: heterogeneous demonstrations and unsupported projections

In this instance the demos ranged from small to very wide grids and used disparate palettes and structures. Observed effects included narrow line fills around isolated markers, multi-directional fills/rays, and replacement of marker cells with 4; one demo also altered a uniform-color region into a rectangular extension. The test had sparse color-3 cells plus color-2 triplets on opposite borders. I inferred a vertical extension from one marker and converted all markers to 4, but that candidate was rejected. The next attempt assumed horizontal fills from the edge runs and was also rejected. These outputs were not validated by exact replay against all demos, and the examples were too heterogeneous to justify transferring that simple rule confidently.

Lesson: after an incorrect submission, do not select one visually appealing delta from the latest demo and apply it to the test. Compare the entire demo set, check dimensions/palette/task-class coherence, and explicitly derive a rule that predicts all changed and unchanged cells. For request decisions, state competing hypotheses and the exact observation needed to distinguish them; additional examples without a specific discriminating purpose are costly but may still fail to make a drifting/non-identifiable mapping coherent. Do not present a speculative grid as evidence-based.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-36d8805c-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/marker-anchor-offsets.md", "content": "# Marker-to-template anchor offsets

When demos show sparse markers receiving copies of an embedded motif, distinguish the motif's bounding-box origin from the motif coordinate aligned with a marker. Represent that alignment as a signed offset and validate it against multiple marker/copy pairs. Replay every marker footprint against the complete demo output, including source motif retention, overlaps, clipping, and unchanged candidate markers.

A common failure is to shift a plausible template by intuition (top-left, center, or a salient source pixel) without validating the anchor. After incorrect feedback, discard that placement hypothesis and re-derive the offset from observed coordinate correspondences. Validate serialization independently: row widths and legal values must be checked before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/marker-anchor-offsets.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md", "content": "# Session review: repeated unvalidated submissions after one demo

In this instance, the only demo had a 29×11 input and a 14×11 output, while the test was 4×9. The demo showed a dense structured field, a full-width separator row (color 4), and a uniform region below it; the output preserved the top 14×11 portion while changing its color-1 cells to color 2. This was strong evidence that the task was not a simple mapping of the test's visible 4×4 color-3 motif to a color-2 motif. The test also contained a full-height color-1 divider and color-3 regions, so its partition/representation needed to be inferred via a discriminating additional demo.

The unsuccessful actions repeatedly submitted variants of the same guessed 4×4 output, including an identical grid more than once, and claimed an overlap rule without demo support. After a wrong submission, reopen task class and output dimensions; do not repeat an identical grid or cosmetic variant. If the one demo does not establish how a differently sized test is represented, spend a demo request to resolve the mapping rather than burn attempts on unsupported guesses. Keep claims about the rule out of the response unless evidence validates them. In puzzle mode, still emit exactly one permitted JSON action on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/marker-anchor-offsets.md", "content": "# Marker-to-template anchor offsets

When demos show sparse markers receiving copies of an embedded motif, keep two coordinate concepts separate: (1) the motif's bounding-box origin and (2) the motif coordinate that aligns with a marker. Represent the latter as a signed offset from the origin and validate it against multiple marker/copy pairs. A plausible colored pattern is not enough: replay every marker footprint against the complete output, including the original motif, overlaps, clipping, and unchanged candidate markers.

A repeated failure mode is to shift the right motif by an intuitively appealing amount (top-left, center, a nearby marker, or a visually salient source pixel) without validating the anchor. On incorrect feedback, discard that placement hypothesis and derive the offset anew from observed coordinate correspondences. Also validate serialization independently: every row must have the expected width and all values must be legal before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u025/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/marker-anchor-offsets.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/failed-guess-reassessment.md", "content": "# Failed-submission reassessment

When a plausible full-grid cleanup or marker-repair guess is incorrect, treat that as evidence against the whole inferred rule, not a cue to make another nearby edit. Reconstruct the exact input/output delta in every demo, including unchanged markers and structures. Separate the observed operation from assumptions about color regions and object boundaries. If multiple rules still fit, name them and request a demo specifically predicted to differ; otherwise submit only a transformation replayed against all available examples. This avoids cascading unsupported guesses and needless penalties.
